  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 334 AM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nThe beginning of some much cooler temperatures and increased \npotential for precipitation will arrive on Tuesday. While much of \nTuesday will remain dry, a developing low pressure system over \nColorado will bring the potential of significant rainfall Tuesday \nnight through Thursday. Latest model runs appear to have slowed this \nsystem down a little with the best potential for rainfall appearing \nto now Wednesday night as the low deepens and pushes off the Front \nRanges into Nebraska. This precipitation potential will be enhanced \nas a secondary wave pushing north out of Texas interacts with the \ndeepening system over the Nebraska/Kansas border. Precipitation will \ncontinue into Thursday before moving off to the east by Thursday \nevening. At this time, QPF amounts are still uncertain, but there is \nstill the potential for some embedded thunderstorms Wednesday \nevening which may produce some heavier rainfall. At this time, not \nexpecting any severe thunderstorms or flooding concerns, but will \ncontinue to monitor over the next few days. \n\nBeyond Thursday night, strong upper level ridging will return to the \nwestern US allowing for surface high pressure to build south out of \nCanada bringing a return to dry conditions through the weekend. With \nthis high, cold air advection will push in a much colder airmass \ninto the northern and central Great Plains. With 850 mb temperatures \nin the -5 to 1 C range on Friday, surface highs will struggle to \nrise out of the 50s. Highs will gradually moderate through next \nweekend, rising back into the 70s by next Sunday. For low \ntemperatures, this CAA will drop overnight temperatures into the mid \nto upper 30s across the Panhandle and northwest Nebraska on Friday \nmorning. Widespread low temperatures in the low to mid 30s are \nexpected for Saturday morning. Given this colder airmass, frost \npotential is possible by the end of the week. At this time, \nheadlines are not in place, but if this colder temperature trend \nremains consistent and confidence increases, they may be necessary. \nStay tuned to the latest forecasts for the most up to date \ninformation regarding frost/freeze potential.",
